<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Important Insight</span>Storage needs in Indian kitchens are heavily underestimated at the planning stage. A useful calculation: for every person in the household, plan for approximately 0.9 running metres of lower cabinet and 0.7 running metres of upper cabinet. A family of four therefore needs around 3.6m of base storage — more than many standard kitchen quotes allow for.</div>
<p>Section 04 — The Big Debate</p>
<h2>Marble vs. Tile: A Complete, Honest Comparison for Indian Kitchens</h2>
<p>Few interior design decisions generate as much debate as <strong>marble versus tile</strong> for kitchen surfaces — whether countertops, backsplashes, or flooring. Both have committed advocates and vocal detractors. This section cuts through the noise with an evidence-based comparison across every dimension that actually matters.</p>
<p>Before diving in, let us be precise about what we are comparing. We are looking at <strong>natural marble slabs and marble-look surfaces</strong> (engineered or porcelain marble-look) against <strong>ceramic and vitrified tiles</strong>. The criteria: durability, maintenance, aesthetics, cost, heat resistance, hygiene, and long-term value.</p>
<h3>Marble: The Premium Natural Choice</h3>
<p>Marble has been the aspirational kitchen surface in India for decades. Walk into any upscale home in Bengaluru, Delhi, or Mumbai and you are likely to find a white Statuario or warm Crema Marfil countertop. The appeal is visceral — the veining is natural, the depth is real, and the feel underhand is cool and smooth.</p>
<p><strong>The varieties that matter for Indian kitchens:</strong> Italian Statuario (white with grey veining — prestigious but most porous), Indian Makrana (historically significant, warm white, used in the Taj Mahal), Carrara (blue-grey veins, popular for backsplashes), and Bottticino (beige with subtle veining — excellent for high-traffic kitchens).</p>
<p>The single most important fact about marble in a kitchen: <strong>it is a porous, reactive stone.</strong> It will etch from acids — lemon juice, tamarind, tomato, vinegar. It will stain from oils and turmeric if not sealed and maintained. For Indian cooking styles involving these ingredients daily, this is not a minor inconvenience. It requires active management.</p>
<h3>Tile: The Practical Workhorse</h3>
<p>Modern tiles — particularly <strong>large-format vitrified and porcelain tiles</strong> — have undergone a revolution in the last decade. Today's tiles can be printed to look nearly indistinguishable from marble, at a fraction of the price and with virtually none of the maintenance demands. Italian-made porcelain slabs now come in 1200×2400mm formats that have eliminated the grout-heavy look that used to define tiled surfaces.</p>
<p><strong>Key tile types for kitchen surfaces:</strong> Ceramic tiles (wall backsplash use only — not suitable as countertops), vitrified tiles (floors and walls, rated by PEI hardness), full-body vitrified tiles (best for flooring, consistent colour through the full thickness), and large-format porcelain slabs (countertop and backsplash use, 12–20mm thick).</p>
<p>The grout line remains the tile's Achilles heel — especially in Indian kitchens where oil and masala spatter is constant. Choosing <strong>epoxy grout over cement-based grout</strong> eliminates most of this problem, as epoxy grout is stain-resistant, non-porous, and highly durable. For countertop tiles, specify minimum grout joints (3mm or less) and always use epoxy.</p>
